@extends('default')
@section('content')
<script server>
/**
 * What the map shows and how to read it: the two things a reader cannot work
 * out by looking, which are what a height stands for and where the numbers
 * are from.
 */
const pageTitle = 'About the map'
const pageDescription = 'How to read Votescape: what the heights and colours mean, how counties are counted, and where the 2026 midterm results come from.'
const canonicalPath = '/about'
</script>

<div class="va-page">
  <header class="va-top">
    <a class="va-brand" href="/" aria-label="Votescape, back to the map">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 48 48" width="26" height="26" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
        <rect x="6" y="22" width="9" height="20" rx="2" fill="#5b9dff" />
        <rect x="19.5" y="6" width="9" height="36" rx="2" fill="#e8dce4" />
        <rect x="33" y="15" width="9" height="27" rx="2" fill="#ff6f66" />
      </svg>
      <span>Votescape</span>
    </a>
    <a class="va-back" href="/">Open the map</a>
  </header>

  <article class="va-article">
    <p class="va-eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Land does not vote. People do.</h1>
    <p class="va-lede">A flat election map is mostly empty country. Votescape stands every county up as a tower, as tall as the votes cast there, so the places where most people live are the places you see.</p>

    <div class="va-grid">
      <section>
        <h2>Height</h2>
        <p>A tower's height is the number of votes cast in that county for the office shown. Los Angeles County towers over the map because close to three million people voted there.</p>
        <p>Switch height to votes per square mile to compare places by how closely packed their voters are. That view uses a square-root scale, or Manhattan alone would be all you could see. The labels always give the real figure.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Colour</h2>
        <p>Colour is the margin between the Democrat and the Republican, as a share of the votes for the two of them. Deep blue and deep red are leads of fifty points or more. The pale middle is a close race.</p>
        <div class="va-scale" role="img" aria-label="Colour scale from a Republican lead of 50 points, through even, to a Democratic lead of 50 points"></div>
        <div class="va-scale-ends"><span>R+50</span><span>Even</span><span>D+50</span></div>
        <p>Grey towers are places where votes were cast, but not between a Democrat and a Republican, so there is no margin to show. Flat ground is a place with no race for that office.</p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>The four views</h2>
      <dl class="va-list">
        <div>
          <dt>House</dt>
          <dd>The only office on every ballot in the country. Where a county is split between districts, its districts are added together.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Senate</dt>
          <dd>The 35 states with a Senate race, including the special elections. The rest lie flat.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Governor</dt>
          <dd>The 36 states that chose a governor.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Top of ticket</dt>
          <dd>Each state by its most prominent race: Senate where there was one, otherwise governor, otherwise the House.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How places are counted</h2>
      <ul class="va-notes">
        <li><b>Alaska</b> is drawn by its 40 state house districts, which is how its results are reported. Its boroughs are not.</li>
        <li><b>Connecticut</b> is drawn by its nine planning regions, which replaced its counties. Town results are added up into them.</li>
        <li><b>Uncontested and one-party races.</b> Where most of a county's House votes were cast in a district without both a Democrat and a Republican, the county is grey.</li>
        <li><b>A missing major party.</b> In a statewide race with no Democrat or no Republican, the leading candidate against the other party takes that side, and is named.</li>
        <li><b>Ranked-choice voting</b> in Alaska and Maine is shown by first choices.</li>
        <li><b>Louisiana</b> voted in all-party primaries. Each side is its party's total.</li>
        <li><b>Washington, D.C.</b> lies flat. It has no senator, no governor and no voting seat in the House.</li>
        <li><b>Kalawao County, Hawaii</b>, with about eighty residents, is counted with Maui County and drawn as part of it.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The numbers</h2>
      <p>These are unofficial results, and they will change. Some races are not called, a few go to runoffs, and no state has certified its count. The map says when its numbers were last updated, in the legend.</p>
      <p>Every number on the map is also in a table: choose Table at the top of the map for each state, or each county in a state, as rows you can read or have read to you. Places finds any county by name.</p>
      <p>The shapes are the U.S. Census Bureau's 2024 cartographic boundary files, laid out in the Albers USA projection, with Alaska and Hawaii moved and resized to fit.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How it is made</h2>
      <p>The map is drawn in your browser by <a href="https://github.com/stacksjs/ts-maps">ts-maps</a>, an open source mapping library, as 3,153 extruded shapes over a single layer of data. The site is built with <a href="https://stacksjs.com">Stacks</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <p class="va-cta"><a class="va-button" href="/">Open the map</a></p>
  </article>
</div>

